\n   Duration  5.84s (transform 1.20s, setup 0ms, collect 5.23s, tests 13.91s, environment 7ms, prepare 2.80s), and  pass.\n\nRelated: #31; benchmark harness introduced in #34.","number":35,"state":"CLOSED","title":"Port benchmark harness from Python to Node.js","url":"https://github.com/petercr/jev-orchestrator/issues/35"},{"body":"The latest issue #80 comparison averaged 79.3 seconds for JEV + Codex and 56.6 seconds for standalone Codex, with two repetitions per setup. That small sample does not establish a general speed advantage. Previous Claude comparisons also had differences in tool access and task scope.\n\nBuild a repeatable comparison of JEV + worker against the same worker run directly, using the new CLI performance summary and a task that includes production code and tests.\n\n…621 tokens truncated…ponent tests still pass.\n- Record worker configuration, phase timings, approvals, retries, validation outcomes, patch, and bounded evidence in a persistent pilot receipt.\n- Report any policy/tooling limitation rather than increasing budgets or weakening protections to finish.\n\nUse the built-in CLI performance summary once available. Do not publish a contribution to the upstream repository as part of the pilot.\n","number":30,"state":"CLOSED","title":"Run a production bug-and-tests pilot on TrusTrove #949","url":"https://github.com/petercr/jev-orchestrator/issues/30"},{"body":"## Problem\n\nThe real-repository pilot against [jspdf-md-renderer #80](https://github.com/JeelGajera/jspdf-md-renderer/issues/80) produced the correct minimal patch, but orchestration needed manual action selection, recovery after a rejected evaluation, and an operator-written completion clarification.\n\nThe patch independently passed `npm test` (296 tests), `npm run typecheck`, and `npm run verify`; the reported warning disappeared. Track the orchestration gaps separately from the successful code change.\n\n## Reproduction and evidence\n\n- Orchestrator: packaged CLI `0.2.0`, built from `3378dfc2d4753a45d67166b2727d10698ded0e92`.\n- Test repository starting commit: `JeelGajera/jspdf-md-renderer@5a910ba627c0ff2014c13076fb9c8a9f4559dcd8`.\n- Evaluation: direct TypeSafe, requested `jev-latest`, served `jev-1.13.0`.\n- Worker: Codex CLI `0.160.0`, `gpt-5.6-terra`, high reasoning; one approved call.\n- Ran the installed binary from the test repository directory, after `npm ci`, with a configured TypeSafe credential:\n\n```sh\nJEV_PROVIDER=typesafe ROUTER_MODEL=jev-latest jev-agent . \\\n  https://github.com/JeelGajera/jspdf-md-renderer/issues/80 --orchestrate\n```\n\nThe task was the issue URL only, without extra file-edit hints.\n\n1. First run recorded `READ_FILE → RUN_TESTS → CALL_CODEX`. Codex renamed the config and updated its TypeScript include. The next evaluation was rejected with `Jev returned invalid evaluation data.`; the CLI exited 1 after recording the worker result.\n2. Independent validation passed all three acceptance commands. The config contents and pre-existing unrelated lockfile modification were preserved.\n3. A fresh run recorded `RUN_TESTS → ASK_USER → FINISH`. It finished only after receiving a clarification containing independently verified acceptance results and a reviewed completion override.\n\nThe failing provider response was not retained, so its exact failing field and root cause are unknown. Do not assume the provider itself was necessarily at fault.\n\n## Gaps to address\n\n### Evaluation failure recovery and diagnostics\n\nA rejected post-worker evaluation terminated the CLI without a terminal failure record for that evaluation. Preserve edits and completed evidence, record a sanitized failure stage/code, and offer bounded, explicit recovery. Any supported continuation must retain iteration/call budgets and failed-approach history.\n\nKeep response validation strict. Recovery must not silently approve an action, retry indefinitely, or execute a rejected recommendation. Diagnostic evidence must not contain credentials or raw upstream error bodies.\n\n### Issue context and completion evidence\n\nThe URL-only task and current snapshot did not give Jev enough context to reliably finish. Manual routing was required; the recovery run needed an operator-written verification summary.\n\nDefine a bounded way to resolve issue context and carry relevant repository findings, task acceptance criteria, and independent validation evidence into evaluation. Treat fetched issue text, repository instructions, and worker claims as untrusted data. Keep access read-only and constrained; do not introduce arbitrary command execution.\n\nImprove context and progress handling rather than lowering confidence thresholds or bypassing `ASK_USER`.\n\n### Repository-aware validation\n\nThe loop chose `npm test`. The issue also required `typecheck` and `verify`, which were run independently by the operator. Inspection currently recognizes `test`, `check`, `typecheck`, `lint`, and `build`, but excludes `verify`.\n\nSupport approved validation candidates that cover the repository's declared verification workflow. Completion should require the relevant independent checks, not merely whichever single script happened to run first.\n\n### Worker sandbox limitations\n\nThe worker reported three test suites blocked by loopback server binding (`listen EPERM`) in its workspace sandbox. All 296 tests passed through independent validation outside that sandbox.\n\nDistinguish reported environment restrictions from demonstrated task failures. Use the approved orchestrator validation layer to establish test results; do not automatically broaden worker permissions or treat worker-reported partial results as passing validation.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] An invalid evaluation after a successful worker attempt produces a sanitized terminal trace/result, preserves edits and evidence, and supports an explicit bounded continuation.\n- [ ] Failure diagnostics identify the validation stage/category without logging secrets or raw provider bodies.\n- [ ] Bounded issue context and verified completion evidence reach Jev without requiring the operator to reconstruct the acceptance summary by hand.\n- [ ] Declared comprehensive validation scripts such as `verify` can be selected through typed, approved candidates; required checks gate completion.\n- [ ] Sandbox-related worker test failures remain unverified until independent validation runs, with clear attribution of the environment limitation.\n- [ ] Offline regressions cover rejected evaluations after edits, continuation budgets, ambiguous recommendations, required multi-check validation, sandbox failures, cancellation, and completion only after passing validation.\n- [ ] Preserve authoritative deterministic policy, manual approval, eight-iteration and two-calls-per-adapter limits, credential redaction, and the prohibition on autonomous arbitrary commands, publication, destructive Git, or out-of-repository writes.\n","number":24,"state":"CLOSED","title":"Improve orchestration recovery, issue context, and validation coverage","url":"https://github.com/petercr/jev-orchestrator/issues/24"},{"body":"Jev evaluation currently depends on Vercel AI Gateway. The first live pilot encountered a model access restriction despite valid authentication and a positive credit balance. Add two alternative evaluation providers so users can select an account that has access to Jev.\n\n## Proposed behavior\n\nSupport three explicitly selectable providers, implementing OpenRouter second and direct TypeSafe AI third:\n\n| Provider | Proposed selection | Credential | Model default |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Vercel AI Gateway | `JEV_PROVIDER=vercel` | `AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY` | Existing `typesafe-ai/jev` |\n| OpenRouter | `JEV_PROVIDER=openrouter` | `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` | Documented Jev model, initially `typesafe/jev-1.13` if using the native Decisions API |\n| TypeSafe AI directly | `JEV_PROVIDER=typesafe` | `TYPESAFE_API_KEY` | `jev-latest`, with a documented pinned-version override |\n\nAn unset selector keeps Vercel as the default. Preserve `ROUTER_MODEL` as the override for the selected provider and document each provider's model namespace. Require only the selected provider's credential. Unknown selectors and missing credentials should produce actionable, credential-safe configuration errors. Mock mode remains offline and keyless.\n\nUse explicit selection for this milestone; automatic cross-provider failover can be a separate feature.\n\n## Implementation scope\n\n- Extract the shared bounded snapshot, questions, and assessment normalization from `src/ai/evaluate.ts`; keep each provider's transport and response parsing behind a small typed boundary returning the repository-owned evaluation result.\n- Preserve all four boolean probability questions and the fixed `nextAction` choice with their existing instructions and criteria. Normalize native Noul yes probabilities and Choice distributions/confidence correctly. Validate required fields, allowed actions, finite probabilities, distributions, and confidence. Missing confidence must never become fabricated confidence or permission to execute.\n- Preserve the existing overall 10-second evaluation deadline, at most one transient retry, and cancellation/late-response protection. SDK retry defaults must not multiply the request budget. Keep AbortSignal outside serializable approved candidates and worker context.\n- Retain bounded input/output handling. Send credentials only to the selected provider's trusted endpoint. Redact all configured provider keys from errors, console output, traces, and worker evidence; do not log raw upstream error bodies.\n- Distinguish invalid credentials from authorization/model-plan restrictions, billing failures, rate limits, unavailable models/services, and malformed responses where the provider supplies enough information. Include an authorization-restriction fixture matching the pilot failure, so a valid key is not reported as necessarily invalid.\n- Add selected provider and requested/served model attribution to CLI results and traces without breaking existing consumers. Preserve the current Vercel behavior and trace compatibility.\n- Update `README.md`, `.env.example`, relevant types/tests, and milestone tracking. Document credential setup, provider-specific models, account access prerequisites, and data-handling differences.\n\nThe deterministic policy remains authoritative: manual approval, eight iterations, two calls per coding adapter, independent validation before FINISH, controlled repair, cancellation cleanup, and traceability all remain intact. This issue changes Jev evaluation access; Codex and Claude worker routing stays as it is.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Vercel works with existing configuration; OpenRouter and direct TypeSafe each work with explicit selection and their own key, without requiring a Vercel key.\n- [ ] Mock mode works without credentials or network requests.\n- [ ] Deterministic tests cover all three providers' successful normalization, confident permitted routes, ambiguity/missing confidence, and premature FINISH requiring validation.\n- [ ] Mocked transport tests cover malformed answers, credential/access/billing errors, rate limits, service errors, timeout, retry limits, cancellation during requests/backoff, and no execution after cancellation.\n- [ ] Tests verify credential redaction and provider/model attribution in results and traces.\n- [ ] `pnpm check`, `pnpm test`, and `pnpm build` pass; installed CLI configuration is smoke-tested.\n- [ ] Separate opt-in live checks verify each new provider using authorized credentials and bounded calls, recording only sanitized provider/model/outcome evidence. Automated tests never require live services.\n\n## Verified API starting points\n\nAs checked on October 3, 2026:\n\n- OpenRouter documents `POST https://openrouter.ai/api/alpha/decisions`, including `typesafe/jev-1.13`. It also offers a TypeSafe-compatible `/api/v1/systemone` path. Choose and document one supported transport; verify model aliases and account eligibility during implementation. [Official OpenRouter Jev guide](https://openrouter.ai/blog/insights/what-is-jev/)\n- TypeSafe documents `POST https://api.typesafe.ai/v1/systemone` with bearer authentication. [API reference](https://docs.typesafe.ai/api)\n- TypeSafe's current stable alias is `jev-latest`, resolving to `jev-1.13.0`; responses identify the served version. [Model reference](https://docs.typesafe.ai/models)\n\nThese are typed decision APIs. Use their evaluation contracts rather than treating Jev as a chat completion model.\n","number":22,"state":"CLOSED","title":"Add OpenRouter and direct TypeSafe AI providers for Jev evaluation","url":"https://github.com/petercr/jev-orchestrator/issues/22"}]
"}}
